---
import Html from "~/components/Page/Html/index.astro";
import { Nav } from "~/components/Page/Nav";
import { Body } from "~/components/Page/Body";
import { Main } from "~/components/Page/Main";
import { Footer } from "~/components/Page/Footer";
import { Header } from "~/components/Typography/Header";
import { Paragraph } from "~/components/Typography/Paragraph";
import "~/index.css";

const bootstrapScript = `set -euxo pipefail

curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf -L https://install.determinate.systems/nix | sh -s -- install linux --init none --no-confirm

export PATH="/nix/var/nix/profiles/default/bin:\${PATH}"

nix profile add nixpkgs#direnv

nix --version
direnv --version

mkdir -p ~/.claude
[ -f ~/.claude/settings.json ] || echo '{}' > ~/.claude/settings.json
jq '.includeCoAuthoredBy = false' ~/.claude/settings.json > ~/.claude/settings.json.tmp \\
  && mv ~/.claude/settings.json.tmp ~/.claude/settings.json`;

const envrc = `#! /bin/sh

watch_file flake.nix
watch_file flake.lock
source <(nix print-dev-env)

export CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=$(sops --decrypt --extract '["cloudflare-account-id"]' secrets.yaml)
export CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=$(sops --decrypt --extract '["cloudflare-api-token"]' secrets.yaml)`;

const flakeNix = `{
  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "nixpkgs";
    systems.url = "github:nix-systems/default";
  };
  outputs = inputs: let
    supportedSystems = import inputs.systems;
    forEachSystem = inputs.nixpkgs.lib.genAttrs supportedSystems;

    overlayForSystem = system: (nixpkgs: _: rec {
      mysite = {
        ci = nixpkgs.writeShellApplication {
          name = "ci";
          runtimeInputs = [nixpkgs.nodejs];
          text = ''
            pnpm exec tsc
            pnpm run format
            pnpm run lint --fix
            pnpm run lint
            pnpm run build
            pnpm test
          '';
        };

        frontend = nixpkgs.writeShellApplication {
          name = "frontend";
          runtimeInputs = [nixpkgs.nodejs];
          text = "pnpm run dev";
        };

        deploy = nixpkgs.writeShellApplication {
          name = "deploy";
          runtimeInputs = [nixpkgs.nodejs];
          text = ''
            pnpm exec tsc
            pnpm run build
            pnpm exec wrangler deploy
          '';
        };

        deployPreview = nixpkgs.writeShellApplication {
          name = "deploy-preview";
          runtimeInputs = [nixpkgs.nodejs];
          text = ''
            pnpm exec tsc
            pnpm run build
            pnpm exec wrangler versions upload --preview-alias dev
          '';
        };

        shell = nixpkgs.mkShell {
          packages = [
            mysite.ci
            mysite.deploy
            mysite.deployPreview
            mysite.frontend
            nixpkgs.nodejs
            nixpkgs.pnpm
            nixpkgs.sops
          ];
        };
      };
    });

    nixpkgs = forEachSystem (system:
      import inputs.nixpkgs {
        inherit system;
        overlays = [(overlayForSystem system)];
      });
  in {
    devShells = forEachSystem (system: {
      default = nixpkgs.\${system}.mysite.shell;
    });
  };
}`;

const stopHookSketch = `# runs after every agent turn, before Claude is allowed to stop
if ! git diff --quiet || ! git diff --cached --quiet; then
  echo "Uncommitted changes. Commit and push first." >&2
  exit 2  # non-zero blocks the turn from ending
fi

if [ -n "$(git ls-files --others --exclude-standard)" ]; then
  echo "Untracked files. Commit and push first." >&2
  exit 2
fi

unpushed=$(git rev-list "origin/$(git branch --show-current)..HEAD" --count)
if [ "$unpushed" -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "$unpushed unpushed commit(s). Push first." >&2
  exit 2
fi`;
---

<Html
  title="There Is No Laptop Anymore — Thoughts"
  og={{
    type: "article",
    title: "There Is No Laptop Anymore",
    description:
      "How I build and ship software entirely from a Claude Code cloud environment, on Nix and Cloudflare, for CA$29.40 a month.",
    publishedTime: new Date(2026, 8, 13),
    tags: ["technology", "infrastructure", "cloudflare", "nix"],
  }}
>
  <Body>
    <Nav client:load current="Thoughts" />
    <Main>
      <article>
        <Header>There Is No Laptop Anymore</Header>
        <Paragraph>
          There is no laptop anymore. I say what I want to Claude from my phone,
          and it does the rest. I haven't opened a terminal in months, and I
          still ship code often. The machine doing the programming is a Claude
          Code environment running in the cloud, one I've configured once: it
          installs Nix on startup, carries my own git identity so commits land
          as mine and not some anonymous bot, and drops into my repo with
          everything already wired up.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          The bootstrap itself is almost nothing. A short script runs once when
          the cloud environment starts: it installs Nix (no init system needed,
          since this is a container, not a full OS), adds direnv on top of it,
          and flips one Claude Code setting so it stops appending a
          "Co-Authored-By: Claude" trailer to my commits. My name and email are
          already the author and committer; I don't need the tool announcing
          itself in every commit message too. That's the entire setup.
          Everything else, the toolchains, the secrets, the deploy scripts,
          lives in the repos themselves, not in this script.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          That last part is the one I care most about. Nix is what makes the
          environment disposable. Each of my repos ships its own{" "}
          <code>flake.nix</code> declaring the exact toolchain it needs: Node, pnpm,
          whatever CLI scripts I've wrapped as first-class commands. Paired with direnv,
          the moment the agent <code>cd</code>s into the project, that toolchain
          is just there. No global installs, no "works on my machine," no drift
          between what I had last week and what I have today. The environment is
          cattle, not a pet. If the cloud session disappears, the next one
          reconstructs itself from the repo in seconds.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          Secrets travel the same way. Every project keeps a sops-encrypted{" "}
          <code>secrets.yaml</code>, dual-encrypted for my own machine and for
          Claude's cloud, checked into the repo like any other file. The cloud
          environment itself carries the matching decryption key as one of its
          own environment variables, set once when I configured it. So a fresh{
            " "
          }
          <code>git clone</code> in that environment already has everything it needs:
          no key file to fetch from somewhere else, no separate secrets manager to
          authenticate against first. Credentials load automatically through direnv
          the moment I'm in the directory. I never paste an API token into a chat,
          never keep one in some untracked <code>.env</code> file I'll inevitably
          lose. The repo is self-contained, secrets included.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          The stack on top of that is deliberately boring. TypeScript, Vite or
          Astro, React for whatever UI the project needs. Static output. The
          "backend," when a project needs one, is a Cloudflare Worker: same
          language, same repo, same deploy step. There's no server to patch, no
          OS to update, no process to keep alive. I write a function, it runs on
          the edge when someone requests it, and it costs nothing when nobody
          does. Workers is just the compute half of it, too. The moment a
          project needs to hold onto something instead of just responding, it's
          the same Cloudflare account, no new vendor to sign up for: R2 for
          object storage, D1 for an actual SQL database, KV for simple key-value
          state, all with the same shape of pricing, generous free tiers,
          nothing to provision ahead of time. Past the free tier it does scale
          with usage like anything else, but the limits are generous enough that
          a personal project doesn't get near them.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          Which gets to the part people usually ask about first: what this
          costs. CA$29.40 a month for the Claude subscription, plus about US$10
          a year per domain, registered through Cloudflare. The code itself
          lives on GitHub, free. The Workers platform running it is on the free
          tier. That's the whole bill. The cloud environment itself is a real
          machine, a Firecracker microVM running Ubuntu 24.04 with 4 vCPUs, 15
          GB of RAM, and tens of gigabytes of disk, and I'm not billed a cent
          for any of it. I'm only metered on tokens, and that's the part that
          still amazes me: actual engineering work, on a real machine, and the
          cost that scales is model usage, not compute. No servers to rent, no
          hosting plans to size, no invoice that scales with traffic I'm not
          getting yet. I used to think of infrastructure as a fixed cost you
          accept before you've proven an idea is worth anything. Now there isn't
          one to accept.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          That changes what a side project actually is. It used to be an idea
          plus a weekend spent provisioning a server, wiring up CI, deciding
          where the database lives, remembering to renew a certificate six
          months later. Now it's just the idea. I describe what I want, the
          agent scaffolds it inside a Nix shell that's already correct, and it
          deploys to a Worker that was never not going to scale. The distance
          between "I thought of this" and "this is live at a real URL" has
          gotten small enough that the infrastructure stopped being a decision I
          make and became a default I inherit.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          I don't think this setup is unusual for what it uses. Nix, Cloudflare
          Workers, sops, direnv are not new. What's changed is that I no longer
          need to be the one operating any of it. The agent reads the same{" "}
          <code>flake.nix</code> and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> I would, and it just runs
          the commands. I don't need a laptop's job to shrink down to anything, because
          I'm just as often on my phone, saying what I want and reading back what
          happened. The actual computer doing the work is one I don't own, don't patch,
          and don't pay for beyond the subscription.
        </Paragraph>

        <Header.Secondary>Under the Hood</Header.Secondary>

        <Paragraph>
          None of this skips a staging step, it just doesn't need a separate
          environment for one. Every deploy first goes out as a Worker Version
          aliased <code>dev</code>, reachable at its own{" "}
          <code>dev-*.workers.dev</code> URL with zero effect on production traffic.
          I look at that URL, and only then does a second, explicit command promote
          it to production. Production is never the first place a change gets seen
          running.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          There's no separate CI service either. When Claude finishes a turn in
          one of my repos, a stop hook runs first, a script the harness runs
          before it will let the agent consider the task done, and checks the
          actual git state: uncommitted changes, untracked files, commits
          sitting local that never got pushed. Find any of those, and it blocks
          with the reason, and the agent has to fix it before it's allowed to
          stop. It's a smaller surface than a real CI pipeline, but it means the
          same rule that would normally live in a GitHub Actions file lives
          right next to the agent doing the work. Roughly:
        </Paragraph>

        <details class="coutline mt-4 rounded-lg">
          <summary
            class="chover-bg cfocus-visible text-ctextdark cursor-pointer rounded-lg px-4 py-3 text-sm font-medium transition"
          >
            stop hook (sketch)
          </summary>
          <pre
            class="text-ctext bg-cbg overflow-x-auto rounded-b-lg px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs"><code>{stopHookSketch}</code></pre>
        </details>

        <Paragraph className="mt-8">
          The cloud environment's own setup is just as small. This runs once,
          when the environment starts:
        </Paragraph>

        <details class="coutline mt-4 rounded-lg">
          <summary
            class="chover-bg cfocus-visible text-ctextdark cursor-pointer rounded-lg px-4 py-3 text-sm font-medium transition"
          >
            cloud environment bootstrap script
          </summary>
          <pre
            class="text-ctext bg-cbg overflow-x-auto rounded-b-lg px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs"><code>{bootstrapScript}</code></pre>
        </details>

        <Paragraph className="mt-8">
          Then direnv takes over per project. Every repo's <code>.envrc</code>{
            " "
          }
          loads the Nix dev shell and decrypts secrets the moment I{" "}
          <code>cd</code> into it:
        </Paragraph>

        <details class="coutline mt-4 rounded-lg">
          <summary
            class="chover-bg cfocus-visible text-ctextdark cursor-pointer rounded-lg px-4 py-3 text-sm font-medium transition"
          >
            .envrc
          </summary>
          <pre
            class="text-ctext bg-cbg overflow-x-auto rounded-b-lg px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs"><code>{envrc}</code></pre>
        </details>

        <Paragraph className="mt-8">
          And the flake declaring that shell is where the actual commands live:
          a dev server, a CI-equivalent script, a deploy, a preview-deploy, so
          the agent runs <code>nix run .#deploy</code>{" "}
          instead of memorizing a project-specific incantation. A trimmed-down version
          of the shape every repo uses:
        </Paragraph>

        <details class="coutline mt-4 rounded-lg">
          <summary
            class="chover-bg cfocus-visible text-ctextdark cursor-pointer rounded-lg px-4 py-3 text-sm font-medium transition"
          >
            flake.nix
          </summary>
          <pre
            class="text-ctext bg-cbg overflow-x-auto rounded-b-lg px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs"><code>{flakeNix}</code></pre>
        </details>

        <Paragraph className="mt-8 text-sm text-gray-600 italic">
          Note: The thoughts, substance, and structure of this article are mine.
          AI worked on the prose, word choice, and connectors to improve clarity
          and articulation.
        </Paragraph>
      </article>
    </Main>
    <Footer />
  </Body>
</Html>
